  "text": "**Working the problem:** $f'(\\theta) = ie^{i\\theta}(1+e^{i\\theta})^2$, so $|f'(\\theta)| = |1+e^{i\\theta}|^2 = 2+2\\cos\\theta$. The perimeter is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}(2+2\\cos\\theta)\\,d\\theta = 4\\pi \\approx 12.566$.\n\n**Screening each solution:**\n\n- **Solution 1:** Computes $|f'| = 2+2\\cos\\theta$ correctly and integrates to $4\\pi$. Its final answer, 12.566370614359172, equals $4\\pi$. The simplicity check is brief but sound: $g'(z)=(1+z)^2$ vanishes only at $z=-1$ on the circle, and the other roots of $z^2+3z+3$ have modulus $\\sqrt{3}>1$. The stated final answer is correct.\n- **Solution 2:** Its derivation correctly reaches $4\\pi$, but the stated final answer is 3. That contradicts its own work, so it is rejected.\n- **Solution 3:** Treats the problem as a Ramsey number $R(K_5, K_5-e)$. This is off-target and unrelated to the curve, so it is rejected.\n- **Solution 4:** Its derivation also correctly reaches $4\\pi$, but the stated final answer is 4. That does not match the derived value, so it is rejected.\n\nOnly Solution 1 has a final answer that matches the correct value $4\\pi$.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
